  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-2)=\u27e80,3,-9\u27e9$ and $g(-2)=\u27e82,0,1\u27e9,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(t)}{3}+2g(t)].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -2}[\\frac{f(x)}{3}+2g(x)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -2}\\frac{f(x)}{3}+{lim}_{t\\to -2}2g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{t\\to -2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to -2}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{t\\to -2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to -2}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}f(-2)+2g(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\u27e80,3,-9\u27e9+2\u27e82,0,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,1,-3\u27e9+\u27e84,0,2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,1,-1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
